# ENIAC

ENIAC (Electronic Numerical Integrator and Computer) was the first programmable, electronic, general-purpose digital computer, completed in 1945. Designed by physicists and engineers John Mauchly and J. Presper Eckert at the University of Pennsylvania, it was primarily funded by the United States Army's Ordnance Corps to calculate complex artillery firing tables during World War II. Unlike earlier electro-mechanical designs that relied on physical relays, ENIAC utilized vacuum tubes for its computational logic, resulting in calculation speeds nearly a thousand times faster than existing electromechanical machines. The computer was Turing-complete and capable of being programmed to solve a wide class of numerical problems, initially requiring the physical rewiring of its patch cables and setting of multi-position switches. Its first highly classified and practical use involved complex mathematical simulations for the hydrogen bomb feasibility study at the Los Alamos Laboratory. ENIAC represented a critical milestone in the history of computing engineering, bridging the gap between mechanical calculators and modern computer architecture. Although its initial design lacked the internal memory capacity and the stored-program flexibility of subsequent systems, its operational success proved the viability of large-scale electronic computing. The machine operated nearly continuously from its public unveiling in 1947 until it was finally decommissioned and powered down in 1955, leaving a foundational legacy on the trajectory of computer science.

## Origins and Development

### Project PX and the Moore School

The conceptual foundation for ENIAC was established in 1941 when John Mauchly, a professor at the University of Pennsylvania's Moore School of Electrical Engineering, drafted a memorandum proposing a general-purpose electronic calculator. In 1943, the United States Army, struggling with a massive backlog of artillery firing tables needed for the war effort, established a contract with the Moore School under the codename "Project PX." The project was placed under the technical direction of J. Presper Eckert as the chief engineer and John Mauchly as the chief consultant, with Herman Goldstine serving as the Army's liaison. Construction took place in secrecy at the Moore School and cost approximately $487,000 (equivalent to several million dollars in modern currency).

### Hardware Architecture

ENIAC was physically massive, occupying 1,800 square feet (167 square meters) and weighing nearly 30 tons. Its architecture utilized approximately 18,000 vacuum tubes, 7,200 crystal diodes, 1,500 relays, 70,000 resistors, and 10,000 capacitors.[1] The system was divided into discrete functional units, including 20 accumulators, which could both add and store ten-digit decimal numbers. Unlike later binary-based computing systems, ENIAC operated on a base-10 decimal system using ten-position ring counters. Synchronization was maintained by a centralized cycling unit that emitted a 100 kilohertz clock signal. Due to the high number of vacuum tubes, tube failure was a significant early concern; however, the engineering team mitigated this by running the tubes below their rated voltage and instituting strict maintenance protocols, eventually achieving an operational uptime of several days between failures.

## Programming and Operation

### Physical Programming

In its original configuration, ENIAC did not possess a stored-program architecture. Programming the machine was a mechanical, labor-intensive process that involved manually mapping mathematical problems onto the hardware. This required routing data and control pulses between the various functional units by plugging cables into patch panels and setting thousands of discrete rotary switches.[2] A single complex program could take days to plan on paper and several additional days to physically configure on the machine, followed by an extensive debugging period.

### The ENIAC Programmers

The complex task of translating mathematical algorithms into the machine's physical configurations was primarily accomplished by a team of six female programmers: Kathleen Antonelli, Jean Bartik, Betty Holberton, Marlyn Meltzer, Frances Spence, and Ruth Teitelbaum.[3] Initially recruited as "human computers" to manually calculate ballistic trajectories, these women developed the first subroutines and software logic required to operate ENIAC. Despite their critical role in determining how the hardware would be functionally utilized, their contributions were largely omitted from the early historical record and public relations materials of the era, only receiving formal recognition decades later.

## Operational History

### Major Computations

Although ENIAC was designed for ballistic tables, its initial real-world test in late 1945 involved calculations for Edward Teller's "Super" (thermonuclear) bomb design. The Los Alamos team, led by Nicholas Metropolis and Stanley Frankel, used the machine to simulate the complex thermodynamic dynamics of an early hydrogen bomb concept. Following its formal dedication in February 1946, the machine was moved to the Aberdeen Proving Ground in Maryland in 1947. Beyond ballistics and weapons research, ENIAC was utilized for early numerical weather prediction models, cosmic ray studies, thermal ignition problems, and random number studies, including the computation of Pi ($\pi$) and *e* to thousands of decimal places.

### The Stored-Program Modification

In 1948, ENIAC underwent a major architectural modification to address the extreme inefficiency of manual rewiring. Influenced by discussions with John von Neumann regarding stored-program concepts, the engineering team wired the machine to use its "Function Tables" (initially read-only memory meant for storing math tables) to store program instructions. While this limited the machine's parallel processing capabilities and reduced its overall execution speed, it reduced the time required to load a new program from days to a matter of hours, vastly improving the system's practical utility for the remainder of its service life.

